#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""bin/lab-verify -- check a REQ-171 L0 lab record offline. It never reports trust.

    python3 -I bin/lab-verify --receipt RECORD.json --registry LAB-KEYS.json --act-hash HEX [--accept-signer LABEL]... [--cosignature-checked-by-caller]

RECORD.json is a lab record's four-field envelope (signature, key_id, signed_payload,
receipt_hash), as POST /v1/execute answers it under "decision" and GET /v1/sanction-requests/:id
under "statement". LAB-KEYS.json is the lab key file (mix requisition.lab.keygen writes it; GET
/v1/keys serves it). HEX is the act hash the checker expects the record to be bound to.

WHAT A LAB RECORD IS, AND WHY THIS NEVER SAYS "VERIFIED". A lab record is a legacy agent_receipt.v1
receipt signed by a DEV key held in the app's environment (REQ-171 L0, the publisher Decision
11e17a20). The best it can be is DEGRADED (lab): one dev key, legacy format, not a trust root. So a
record that passes every check here exits 5, never 0, and the unchanged verifier's exit 0 is never
quoted without this verdict (the plan's "What must not be claimed").

THE TWO PATHS, chosen by the lab key file's entry for the record's key_id (its "algorithm"; an entry
without one, or with any other value, is refused: default deny):

  * ed25519 -- the record goes, as read, to the UNCHANGED stdlib verifier
    bench/holytrinity-v2/verifier/verify_receipt.py (released by tag; this file never edits or
    copies it), run as a stranger runs it: python3 -I verify_receipt.py --receipt --registry. Any
    verdict of its other than exit 0 is passed through UNCHANGED, its exit and its words, so its
    codes stay its own (registry_missing_key for a production record checked against the lab key
    file, L0-D7). On its exit 0 the lab checks below run, and a record that passes them exits 5
    lab_dev_key.
  * mldsa87 -- verify_receipt.py is NOT run: it checks Ed25519 only, by its own rule (G-212,
    verify_receipt.py:36-42), and requires a 32-byte key (:340). Python's standard library has no
    ML-DSA, so this file checks the record's bytes, binding and labels and the key entry's own
    integrity, never the signature, and exits 5 lab_signature_unchecked_mldsa87, saying so. The
    signature is checked by mix requisition.lab.verify_mldsa87 ENVELOPE --keys LAB-KEYS.json (Erlang
    :crypto over OpenSSL 3.5; not an independent implementation, GAP L0-G1).

A record whose key_id the key file does not name, or names twice, goes to the unchanged verifier
on either path: those are its trust questions, answered in its words.

THE KEY RULE (2026-11-11 crypto review, W1-1 and W1-4). One rule for when a key may sign, in every verifier
(lib/autonomous_agency/authority/execute_api/lab_key_rule.ex is its text; lab/requisition_key_rule_vectors.v1.json
its vectors, run against this file and the others): a key is refused if it is compromised or revoked (key_revoked),
if its status is not active or retired (key_status_unusable), if its window is unreadable, if the record's signed
occurred_at is unreadable, or if occurred_at is outside valid_from <= occurred_at < valid_to (key_not_valid_at_signing);
a retired key verifies exactly its own window. This file applies the rule ITSELF, once (apply_key_rule), on both
paths and BEFORE the unedited verify_receipt.py, which does not know a signer, treats a retired key with no valid_to as
open-ended and accepts an instant with a trailing newline (lab_key_revoked, lab_key_status_not_active,
lab_key_window_unreadable, lab_signed_instant_unreadable, lab_key_not_valid_at_signing, lab_signer_not_accepted; exit 5,
a trust refusal); what the rule passes the reference then judges in its own words and codes. A record signed by a key whose status
is "compromised" is therefore refused lab_key_revoked (exit 5) here, no longer passed through as the reference's exit 6.
--accept-signer LABEL (repeatable, never empty: an empty accepted-signers set is a configuration error) is the relying
party's policy: a record whose key file entry's signer is not one of the labels is refused lab_signer_not_accepted.
Without it any signer verifies, as before: --accept-signer kms makes KMS custody bound what verifies (the dev
keys are exportable). The inputs are read STRICTLY (W1-6): a duplicated member, NaN/Infinity, a number outside the
double range, a byte-order mark, bytes that are not UTF-8, nesting deeper than 64 containers or an integer outside
+-(2^53-1) are refused (exit 2); the released verifier, which reads
leniently, is only ever handed bytes this file has already read strictly.

CO-SIGNATURES (G-627). This file checks the Ed25519 signature only. A record that carries an ML-DSA-87
co-signature (a "cosignature" member beside the envelope) or announces one (signed metadata.cosign.status
"signed") is said so on every verdict that passes: "co-signature present, NOT checked by this tool (use
verify_offline --require-pq)". The exit code is unchanged. A caller that checks the co-signature itself (verify_offline.py)
passes --cosignature-checked-by-caller and the line is not printed: it would sit inside that caller's PASS, which did check it.

THE CHECKS, in order (an accusation needs standing, so it waits until the key file names the key):
  1. the inputs: two readable JSON files, the envelope's four fields strings, an act hash given;
  2. the key file's entry for the key_id, exactly one (else the verifier, above), its algorithm;
  3. receipt_hash is the SHA-256 of signed_payload, compared here before anything else accuses the
     record: the unchanged verifier reads the signed occurred_at first (verify_receipt.py:378-387
     before :394-404), so a one-byte change that breaks the JSON or the instant reaches it as a
     trust refusal (exit 5), and every one-byte change must exit 1 (L0-D2, L0-M12);
  4. the path's own check: the verifier (ed25519), or the entry's status, fingerprint, key length and
     the signature's form (mldsa87);
  5. the signed payload is a JSON object whose receipt_type is a lab kind (requisition.lab.), or an
     effector allow's (REQ-189 R4-3: receipt_type the act type, subject_type effector, signed
     metadata.record_kind requisition.lab.decision.v0);
  6. its signed metadata carries the four labels the Decision requires (profile lab, signer dev,
     n6_profile not_followed, hands one-person (G-301)), or, for a record a lab KMS seat signed
     (K1), the same four with signer kms;
  7. the signed metadata.key_id equals the envelope's key_id (L0-D5), and it is one of the two lab
     dev key ids (LabSigner's seat table; a pin compares the two); or, for signer kms (K1): the key
     file's entry says signer kms, the key id is KMS_KEY_ID_PREFIX[the entry's algorithm] + a
     resource id (LabKms.key_id_prefixes/0; a pin compares the two), and the signed
     metadata.kms_key_arn is a KMS key ARN ending in key/<that resource id>;
  8. the signed metadata.act_hash equals --act-hash, byte for byte (L0-D2): only a statement or a
     decision binds an act, so a registration or grant record is refused here.

A RECORD SIGNED BY A LAB KMS KEY (K1) is checked exactly as above on its path. A record that passes
is still DEGRADED (lab) and exits 5: on the Ed25519 path with verdict_code lab_kms_key (the
record's signed signer is kms; lab_dev_key is a signer-dev record's only, review 2026-11-10 item 8),
on the ML-DSA-87 path with lab_signature_unchecked_mldsa87 as for any record there. The message says
ONLY what this file can establish offline (review item 2): the record was signed by a lab key the
key file given pins as AWS KMS key <kms_key_arn> (signer kms), its signature checked against that
pinned public key (on the ML-DSA-87 path: by mix requisition.lab.verify_mldsa87, not here); that
the private key is held in AWS KMS and never exported is the key file's statement, not checked.

EXIT CODES (unchanged by the key rule, the signer policy and the co-signature note): 0 (a CEASE record that passed: RECORDED (no authority), lab_recorded_no_authority; REQ-189
R2-4), 5 (a lab record that passed: lab_dev_key, lab_kms_key or lab_signature_unchecked_mldsa87; or a trust
refusal: the verifier's, or lab_key_* / lab_signed_instant_unreadable on the ML-DSA-87 path (THE KEY RULE), or lab_signer_not_accepted
(--accept-signer), or lab_verifier_unavailable), 1 (a
refusal of the record or of an entry without an algorithm: every lab_ code below), 2 (usage),
and whatever else the unchanged verifier answered, passed through (1, 2 or 5). Every verdict
ends with one line "verdict_code: <code>". Codes of this file are its own, each lab_-prefixed,
sharing no spelling with the verifier's, a step-0 code or a DenialTaxonomy code (L0-A3).

STANDARD LIBRARY ONLY, AND THE IMPORT LIST BELOW IS THE WHOLE OF WHAT THIS FILE MAY REACH; a test
pins it exactly (test/autonomous_agency_web/v1/lab_verify_pins_test.exs). Nothing here signs, and
nothing here verifies a signature itself.
"""

import base64
import hashlib
import json
import os
import subprocess
import sys
import tempfile

EXIT_LAB, EXIT_REFUSED, EXIT_USAGE = 5, 1, 2
# REQ-189 (R2-4, D5): a CEASE record that passed: it authorises nothing, so there is no trust to
